{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0010","statement":"The paper introduces a model for the linguistic hedges 'very' and 'quite' within the label semantics framework, combined with the prototype and conceptual spaces theories of concepts.","source_span":"We introduce a model for the linguistic hedges `very' and `quite' within the label semantics framework, and combined with the prototype and conceptual spaces theories of concepts."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0010","statement":"Hedges are words such as 'very', 'quite', 'more or less', and 'extremely'.","source_span":"Hedges are words such as `very', `quite', `more or less', `extremely'."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0010","statement":"'Very short' applies to fewer objects than 'short', and 'quite short' applies to more objects than 'short'.","source_span":"the term `very short' applies to fewer objects than `short', and `quite short' to more."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0010","statement":"Linguistic hedges have been widely applied in fuzzy classifiers and database queries.","source_span":"Linguistic hedges have been widely applied, including in fuzzy classifiers BIBREF4 , BIBREF5 , BIBREF6 , BIBREF7 and database queries BIBREF8 , BIBREF9 ."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0010","statement":"Label semantics is a random set approach that quantifies an agent's subjective uncertainty about the extent of application of a concept, called semantic uncertainty.","source_span":"This is a random set approach which quantifies an agent's subjective uncertainty about the extent of application of a concept. We refer to this uncertainty as semantic uncertainty"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0010","statement":"Label semantics is combined with conceptual spaces and prototype theory to formalize concepts as based on a prototype and a threshold, located in a conceptual space.","source_span":"the label semantics approach is combined with conceptual spaces BIBREF14 and prototype theory BIBREF15 , to give a formalisation of concepts as based on a prototype and a threshold, located in a conceptual space."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0010","statement":"Conceptual spaces were proposed by Gärdenfors as a framework for representing information at the conceptual level.","source_span":"Conceptual spaces are proposed by Gärdenfors in BIBREF14 as a framework for representing information at the conceptual level."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0010","statement":"Conceptual spaces are geometrical structures based on quality dimensions such as weight, height, hue, and brightness.","source_span":"conceptual spaces are geometrical structures based on quality dimensions such as weight, height, hue, brightness, etc."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0010","statement":"Dimensions are integral if assigning a value in one implies a value in another (e.g., depth and breadth); separable dimensions have no such implication (e.g., height and sweetness).","source_span":"Dimensions are integral if assignment of a value in one dimension implies assignment of a value in another, such as depth and breadth. Conversely, separable dimensions are those where there is no such implication, such as height and sweetness."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0010","statement":"Fuzzy set theory was extended to linguistic variables, i.e. variables taking words as values rather than numbers.","source_span":"extended these ideas in BIBREF2 to linguistic variables as variables taking words as values, rather than numbers."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0010","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What words does the paper give as examples of linguistic hedges?","answer":"The paper lists 'very', 'quite', 'more or less', and 'extremely' as examples of linguistic hedges."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0010","fact_refs":[2],"question":"According to the paper, how does 'very short' compare to 'short' in terms of the objects it applies to?","answer":"'Very short' applies to fewer objects than 'short', while 'quite short' applies to more objects than 'short'."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0010","fact_refs":[0,5],"question":"What theoretical frameworks does the paper's hedge model combine with label semantics?","answer":"The model combines label semantics with the prototype theory and conceptual spaces theories of concepts."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0010","fact_refs":[6,7],"question":"Who proposed conceptual spaces and what are they based on?","answer":"Gärdenfors proposed conceptual spaces as a framework for representing information at the conceptual level; they are geometrical structures based on quality dimensions such as weight, height, hue, and brightness."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0010","fact_refs":[8],"question":"What is the difference between integral and separable dimensions in conceptual spaces, per the paper's examples?","answer":"Integral dimensions imply each other's assignment, like depth and breadth; separable dimensions have no such implication, like height and sweetness."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0010","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What does the label semantics framework quantify, and what is this uncertainty called?","answer":"It is a random set approach that quantifies an agent's subjective uncertainty about the extent of application of a concept, referred to as semantic uncertainty."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0011","statement":"The chapter describes the Grail theorem prover, a series of freely available tools for designing and testing grammars in various modern type-logical grammars.","source_span":"This chapter describes the Grail theorem prover, a series of tools for designing and testing grammars in various modern type-logical grammars which functions as a tool . All tools described in this chapter are freely available."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0011","statement":"The Grail family of theorem provers works with multimodal type-logical grammars, NL_cl, the Displacement calculus, and hybrid type-logical grammars.","source_span":"The Grail family of theorem provers have been designed to work with a variety of modern type-logical frameworks, including multimodal type-logical grammars BIBREF0 , NL $_{cl}$ BIBREF1 , the Displacement calculus BIBREF2 and hybrid type-logical grammars BIBREF3 ."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0011","statement":"Type-logical grammars originated when Lambek introduced his Syntactic Calculus, later called the Lambek calculus (L) by later authors.","source_span":"Type-logical grammars originated when BIBREF4 introduced his Syntactic Calculus (called the Lambek calculus, L, by later authors)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0011","statement":"Lambek's main innovation was casting the calculus as a logic, giving a sequent calculus and showing decidability by means of cut elimination.","source_span":"Lambek's main innovation was to cast the calculus as a logic, giving a sequent calculus and showing decidability by means of cut elimination."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0011","statement":"The Lambek calculus's atomic formulas normally include s for sentence, n for common noun, and np for noun phrase.","source_span":"The simplest formulas used in the Lambek calculus are atomic formulas, which normally include $s$ for sentence, $n$ for common noun, $np$ for noun phrase."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0011","statement":"A sentence w1,...,wn is grammatical iff A1,...,An ⊢ C is provable in the logic, for some Ai in lex(wi) and some goal formula C.","source_span":"A sentence $w_1, \\ldots , w_n$ is grammatical iff the statement $A_1,\\ldots , A_n \\vdash C$ is provable in our logic, for some $A_i \\in \\textit {lex}(w_i)$ and for some goal formula $C$ ."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0011","statement":"The word 'the' is assigned the formula np/n in the lexicon, indicating it looks for a common noun to its right to form a noun phrase.","source_span":"a word like “the” which is assigned the formula $np/n$ in the lexicon, indicating that it is looking for a common noun (like “student”) to its right to form a noun phrase"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0011","statement":"The bridge between syntax and semantics in type-logical grammars is the Curry-Howard isomorphism between linear lambda terms and proofs in multiplicative intuitionistic linear logic.","source_span":"The “bridge” between syntax and semantics in the figure is the Curry-Howard isomorphism between linear lambda terms and proofs in multiplicative intuitionistic linear logic."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0011","statement":"Lambek introduced his calculus as a way to obtain an effective rule or algorithm for distinguishing sentences from nonsentences, applicable to formal and natural languages.","source_span":"Lambek introduced his calculus as a way to “obtain an effective rule (or algorithm) for distinguishing sentences from nonsentences”, which would be applicable both to formal and to (at least fragments of) natural languages"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0011","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"What is the Grail theorem prover and what type-logical frameworks was it designed to work with?","answer":"The Grail theorem prover is a series of freely available tools for designing and testing type-logical grammars, designed to work with multimodal type-logical grammars, NL_cl, the Displacement calculus, and hybrid type-logical grammars."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0011","fact_refs":[2,3],"question":"Who introduced the Syntactic Calculus that later became known as the Lambek calculus, and what was his main innovation?","answer":"Lambek introduced the Syntactic Calculus, later called the Lambek calculus (L); his main innovation was casting it as a logic, giving a sequent calculus and showing decidability via cut elimination."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0011","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What atomic formulas are normally used in the Lambek calculus?","answer":"The Lambek calculus normally uses s for sentence, n for common noun, and np for noun phrase as its atomic formulas."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0011","fact_refs":[6],"question":"In the Lambek calculus example, what formula is assigned to the word 'the' and what does it mean?","answer":"The word 'the' is assigned the formula np/n, meaning it looks for a common noun to its right to produce a noun phrase."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0011","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What connects syntax and semantics in the standard architecture of type-logical grammars?","answer":"The Curry-Howard isomorphism between linear lambda terms and proofs in multiplicative intuitionistic linear logic serves as the bridge between syntax and semantics."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0011","fact_refs":[8],"question":"Why did Lambek originally introduce his calculus?","answer":"He introduced it as a way to obtain an effective rule or algorithm for distinguishing sentences from nonsentences, applicable to both formal languages and fragments of natural languages."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0012","statement":"The paper proposes SpCoA, a nonparametric Bayesian spatial concept acquisition method that integrates a generative model for self-localization with unsupervised word segmentation of uttered sentences via latent variables related to spatial concepts.","source_span":"The proposed method is nonparametric Bayesian spatial concept acquisition method (SpCoA) that integrates the generative model for self-localization and the unsupervised word segmentation in uttered sentences via latent variables related to the spatial concept."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0012","statement":"SpCoA was implemented on SIGVerse, a simulation environment, and on TurtleBot2, a mobile robot in a real environment.","source_span":"We implemented the proposed method SpCoA on SIGVerse, which is a simulation environment, and TurtleBot2, which is a mobile robot in a real environment."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0012","statement":"The robot addressed in the study has no prior knowledge of words except for a primitive acoustic model.","source_span":"We address the problem of learning novel words by a robot that has no prior knowledge of these words except for a primitive acoustic model."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0012","statement":"Experimental results showed SpCoA enabled the robot to acquire the names of places from speech sentences and reduce the uncertainty in self-localization.","source_span":"The experimental results showed that SpCoA enabled the robot to acquire the names of places from speech sentences. They also revealed that the robot could effectively utilize the acquired spatial concepts and reduce the uncertainty in self-localization."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0012","statement":"The paper gives 'aporu', 'epou', and 'aqpuru' as example incorrect phoneme recognition results for the word 'apple'.","source_span":"if a robot obtains the speech recognition results “aporu”, “epou”, and “aqpuru” (incorrect phoneme recognition of apple)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0012","statement":"The example speech recognition result 'thisizanaporu' must be segmented by the robot into individual words such as 'this', 'iz', 'an', and 'aporu'.","source_span":"a robot obtains a speech recognition result, such as “thisizanaporu.” The robot has to necessarily segment a sentence into individual words, e.g., “this”, “iz”, “an”, and “aporu”."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0012","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What is SpCoA and what does it integrate?","answer":"SpCoA is a nonparametric Bayesian spatial concept acquisition method that integrates a generative model for self-localization with unsupervised word segmentation of uttered sentences via latent variables related to spatial concepts."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0012","fact_refs":[1],"question":"On what platforms was SpCoA implemented and tested?","answer":"SpCoA was implemented on SIGVerse, a simulation environment, and on TurtleBot2, a mobile robot operating in a real environment."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0012","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What prior knowledge does the robot have about words before learning, according to the paper?","answer":"The robot has no prior knowledge of words except for a primitive acoustic model."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0012","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What did the experimental results show about SpCoA's effect on self-localization?","answer":"SpCoA enabled the robot to acquire the names of places from speech sentences and to effectively use the acquired spatial concepts to reduce uncertainty in self-localization."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0012","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What example does the paper give of incorrect phoneme recognition results for the word 'apple'?","answer":"The paper gives 'aporu', 'epou', and 'aqpuru' as example incorrect phoneme recognitions of the word 'apple'."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0012","fact_refs":[5],"question":"In the paper's sentence segmentation example, how does 'thisizanaporu' get segmented into words?","answer":"It gets segmented into 'this', 'iz', 'an', and 'aporu'."}
